PR #1586: Store response.json() on turns, condensed with condense-json

[MERGED by simonw]

Claude Code (Fable 5) prompt:

> We no longer store `response.json()` in the database - we used to do that until a few weeks ago. This means we lose some information. We used to use `condense-json` for that, in fact we still keep that as a dependency even though it is no longer used.
>
> Bump that dependency to `>=1.0` (though none of those changes should matter much to us) - then experiment with how that library could help us store the full `response.json()` in our database while avoiding duplicated data that is otherwise stored in our various parts. We are trying NOT to store duplicate data if we can avoid it, in fact that's why condense-json was first invented back in 2025.

<details><summary>Claude Code PR</summary>

## What this does

Restores per-call storage of the raw provider payload, which was dropped entirely when logging moved to the message store. The payload lands in a new nullable `turns.response_json` column, **condensed** with [condense-json](https://github.com/simonw/condense-json) so nothing the parts tables already hold is stored twice.

- **Write:** strings of 64+ characters that appear in the turn's own messages — response text, reasoning summaries and their encrypted blobs, long tool arguments, tool output, provider metadata (walked recursively) — are replaced with `{"$": "0.0.text"}`-style references keyed by message offset / part position / field path.
- **Read:** the replacement dict is never stored; it is rebuilt from the chain segment between the turn's parent and tip, which is sound because message content is hash-frozen. `llm logs --json` includes the resolved payload (no cli.py changes needed — it already decodes `*_json` keys), and `LogStore.turn_response_json(turn_id)` is the Python API. A payload whose references no longer resolve is absent from listings but raises `UncondenseError` from the API.
- **Migration:** `m027_turns_response_json`. Old turns and models with no raw payload record NULL.

What remains in the column is roughly the provider envelope — ids, usage, fingerprints, logprobs, settings echoes — which is precisely the data that had been lost. Measured on live calls: a 400-word gpt-4o-mini answer condenses 3,288 B → 473 B (86%); short answers run 42–48%. The dependency bump to `condense-json>=1.0` is what makes this safe on arbitrary payloads: marker-shaped input (`{"$": ...}`) is escaped via `$raw` for a guaranteed lossless round trip, and unknown references raise a typed `UncondenseError`.

## Streamed reasoning fix

Streamed Responses API calls stored *different* ciphertexts of the same reasoning in the part metadata (harvested at `response.output_item.done`) and in `response_json` (from `response.completed`) — OpenAI encrypts per event. The plugin now re-emits reasoning `provider_metadata` from the final payload after the stream ends, so both records agree on one blob. Verified live on streamed gpt-5-mini, sync and async: condensation savings go from 15% to ~50%.

**Reviewer note — the one subtle mechanism in this PR:** the refresh event targets the reasoning part by retaining a reference to the StreamEvent yielded at `output_item.done`; the framework resolves `part_index` by mutating that event in place before the generator resumes, so by `response.completed` the plugin can read the resolved index back off it. This relies on the documented lockstep between `execute()` and `_process_chunk`.

## Deliberately not covered

- `prompt_json` stays unrecorded — the same mechanism would apply, with fragments as the replacement pool, but that is a separate change.
- Streamed chat-completions payloads still lack `tool_calls` (`combine_chunks` never carried them); the parts remain the richer record on that path.
- No changelog entry yet — `docs/changelog.md` has unrelated uncommitted work in the working tree, so the entry is left to fold into the 0.32 notes.

## Testing

988 tests pass. 16 new: condensed storage and round trips (including marker-shaped payloads), threshold behavior, corrupted-reference handling, replacement-key pinning, and an SSE-stream test where `response.completed` deliberately carries a different ciphertext than `output_item.done`. Also verified live end-to-end against gpt-4o-mini and gpt-5-mini (streamed and not, sync and async).

</details>

