I think we need to store these in the DB, even though they can be really big (that single response was 41MB!)

Comment by simonw: 41MB is **so big** though. My entire `logs.db` database right now is 392M and it has 6,025 responses dating back to 2023-07-11.

Just 10 responses of that size would double my database on disk?

Storing those images as BLOB values and not base64 might help a bit.

Comment by simonw: ```bash
curl -L 'https://gist.github.com/simonw/55894032b2c60b35f320b6a166ded493/raw/c16dbdbf68d4a0b1993c59b99c815a454f844294/recipe.json' | \
  jq '.candidates[0].content.parts' | sqlite-utils insert /tmp/base64.db parts -
```
Produced a 41MB database even after vacuum.

So I converted to blob binary data like this:

```bash
sqlite-utils add-column /tmp/base64.db parts imageBlob blob
sqlite-utils convert /tmp/base64.db parts inlineData '  

import base64, json

def convert(value):
    if not value:
        return value
    d = json.loads(value)
    bd = d["data"]
    return base64.b64decode(bd)
' --output imageBlob
sqlite-utils transform /tmp/base64.db parts --drop inlineData
sqlite-utils vacuum /tmp/base64.db
```
Which did save space, that 41MB database is now 31MB. So about a 25% saving not storing the base64 data.

Comment by simonw: Part of the problem here is that the PNG images returned by that API are pretty huge - 1MB or more:

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/af28c8b3-305c-4d6b-9dbc-f68035b75ddc)

Comment by simonw: Some options to consider:

1. Store them in BLOB columns in the database. Pros: Everything is in one place. Cons: takes up a ton of space, inconvenient to access. Could have an easy command for monitoring storage used and deleting stale images.
2. Store them on the filesystem, store filename in database. Pros: easier to access with other tools, easy to see them and delete them using Finder etc. Cons: can get separated from database more easily.
3. User decides - with a global setting or on a per-prompt basis. Pros: puts user in control. Cons: more complex, do users really want to think about that?
4. Storage via a plugin hook. Pros: users could run a plugin that stashes their images somewhere with infinite space like S3 or R2. Cons: complex, most users won't have API keys for that kind of storage.

